          "text": "{\"translation_en\":\"ROXANE:\\n  Oh! I am very fond of you. I must be going.\\n  (She quickly puts her mask back on, arranges a lace veil over her forehead, and says absent-mindedly):\\n  But you have not told me about the battle\\n  Last night. Truly, it must have been extraordinary!...\\n  —Tell him to write to me.\\n  (She blows him a little kiss from her hand):\\n  Oh! **I love you!**\",\"larger_context_summary\":\"Roxane is speaking to Cyrano, her cousin and trusted confidant, after revealing her romantic interest in the handsome young cadet Christian. She has asked Cyrano to protect Christian and now wants him to encourage Christian to write to her. As she departs, she affectionately kisses her hand to Cyrano and repeats that she loves him. The immediate contrast between this warmth and her preoccupation with Christian makes her meaning primarily familial, friendly, and grateful rather than romantic. The audience also knows that Cyrano himself secretly loves Roxane, so her innocent affection has painful dramatic irony and helps set in motion his role as intermediary between her and Christian.\",\"interpretive_commentary\":\"The first-person present-tense declaration straightforwardly presents Roxane’s affection as genuine, which makes its strong truth-conditional force plausible. Its exclamatory form, repetition, and accompanying hand-kiss increase emotional intensity, but intensity is not the same as an independently reflexive or expressive act: she is still chiefly telling Cyrano how she feels. Nor does the sentence itself promise future conduct, establish a new relationship, or otherwise perform a commitment. Context importantly narrows the kind of love asserted. Roxane has just asked Cyrano to communicate with Christian, the man she desires romantically, so her words to Cyrano convey warm familial or friendly fondness and gratitude. The line is sincere rather than deceptive, although the wider love triangle gives it dramatic irony: Roxane does not know that Cyrano loves her. That irony affects the audience’s experience and the consequences for Cyrano, not the declaration’s core semantic force.\"}"
